## Inputs

Working dir, request, design and deltas, implementation log, commit
range{% if not stack %} — one (repository, base, HEAD) triple per repository of the scope the
round touched —{% endif %}, the range of rounds or increments to attend to, round type —
intermediate or final — and target report path.

List the working dir yourself: artifacts are named by phase, round, wave and
rework attempt, and their names tell you what *should* exist. Read everything
in range — reviewer notes, dispositions, verdicts, escalations,
clarifications, triage rulings, the log, the spec.

You may read source and run read-only version-control commands to check a
claim: that a "Fixed" landed, a commit exists, a frozen document is unchanged.
Spot-checks, not a substitute for reading the artifacts. You delegate nothing
— this is a reading job, and you hold it all together.

## The hunt

This is the list, not a starting point for general critique.

**1. Things that should have been surfaced to a human and were not.** The log
and dispositions are read by the next agent, not the user, so a real problem
written there has been filed, not surfaced. Look for text describing
something an owner would want to decide on, with no escalation or
clarification document behind it: the design assumed something untrue, a
pre-existing bug was worked around, something "will break but is out of
scope", a design item was quietly narrowed or dropped, a workaround was chosen
because the honest fix was too big. The tell is a load-bearing admission in a
passing sentence — or a request constraint the design dropped with no
**Request assessment** entry and no gate ruling behind it. Three cases here
are stops (**The decision**):

- a commit or edit in a repository outside the scope;
- a workaround for a defect in an owned repository with no escalation behind
  it (**Repositories in scope**);
- an override — a pin between repositories replaced by the local checkout —
{% if stack %}
  still in a repository at its ship point.
{% else %}
  that will still be in a repository at its ship point: one the design's
  **Scope** section does not declare with its restoration edit, so no pin
  step at the ship gate removes it.
{% endif %}

**2. Deviations from the design.** Work in the log or commits tracing to
neither the design nor an approved delta. And the mirror: design items with
nothing accounting for them, on a round claiming to be finished.

**3. Deviations from the workflow.** A review wave that never ran; a respond
step skipped though that wave reported findings; a verdict with no
dispositions behind it; a rework verdict with no rework attempt; a delta
implemented against with no review or no user gate; a reused or overwritten
artifact filename; a changed frozen document; commits in range nobody
reviewed. A reviewer that legitimately found nothing skips its responder and
judge while keeping its notes file — not a deviation.

**4. Accept-by-default where adversarial was required.** This system runs on
agents willing to say no. A reviewer finding nothing, or near nothing, on a
substantial diff. Findings in notes that vanish with no disposition and no
mention in the verdict. "Fixed" with no location, or whose named fix is not
in the code. A Won't-Do that is "out of scope" or "I disagree" in costume. A
deferral failing the rubric — worth doing, doable now without a design cycle
or human input. A judge approving a disputed item without engaging the
dispute, or adopting the responder's severity verbatim. A responder agreeing
with every finding.

**5. Anything else that looks wrong** in how the workflow ran. The four above
are where problems hide, not a fence.

### Not your job

Do not re-review the code, hunt bugs, second-guess design decisions on merit,
or propose improvements. If the design says X and the workflow correctly built
X, you have nothing to say about X. You check that the process caught what it
should have; you do not redo its work.

