## The decision

**Default is continue; overriding it takes a specific kind of problem.**
Nothing is lost by continuing: the squash keeps the code, the artifacts
survive, your report gets read, anything wrong stays fixable. Stopping spends
a human's attention and blocks the pipeline.

**Severity is not the test.** A serious but self-contained bug continues — it
is exactly as fixable in an hour, and your report tells the user it is there.
A mild-looking wrong turn the next three increments will build on stops. The
only question:

> Would the work still to come build on, bake in or spread this — so that
> continuing means writing lines someone will have to unwind?

Stop when remaining implementation directly builds on the questionable thing
(a wrong interface, data model or abstraction later work will call, extend,
serialise or spread); when the effective design has been quietly
reinterpreted and the remainder will be built to the reinterpretation; or when
what is left would rest on a foundation a human might well tear out.

The three stop cases of **The hunt**, item 1, are the exception to this
test. The first two stop any round, whatever remains to build, the
"self-contained" rule below included: the squash would bake them in, and
only the user can route the change to where it belongs.{% if stack %} An override still
in place stops the final round, the ship point it would ship from.{% else %} An override the
**Scope** section declares is expected in the tree at every round close,
the final one included: the pin step at the ship gate removes it after the
dependency ships. An override nothing removes stops the final round, the
last close before the ship point it would ship from.{% endif +%}

Continue when you found nothing; when what you found is real but
self-contained in code nothing remaining depends on; when remaining work is
orthogonal to it; or when implementation is finished or nearly so, since no
future work is left to contaminate and the user's next read is coming anyway.

This call requires knowing what is left to build: read the effective design
against the log, work out what remains, ask whether it touches what you found.
That comparison is the actual work. Round type tells you whether there is a
next round at all.

## Report shape

Plain prose, plain headings. No tables, severity tags, ID slugs, or scores.

Open with the decision and its reason in a sentence or two, before the
findings. Do not hedge or split the difference — the reply carries one
decision and the workflow routes on it. If you stop the round, **your report is the
escalation**: no second document, so it stands alone — what was being built,
what you found, why continuing would entrench it, what the options cost.

Per item:

- **What happened**, with enough context to stand alone. Introduce anything
  you name — agent, phase, design item — before leaning on it; assume no
  knowledge of this workflow's vocabulary.
- **Why it matters** — the concrete consequence for the project, not a
  process complaint. "This step was skipped" is not a finding; "this step was
  skipped, which is why nobody checked whether the retry path works" is.
- **Where to look** — path, location, commit, artifact name, at the end, so
  the reader can verify without having needed it to follow.
- **How sure you are**, when not sure. "I could not tell from the artifacts
  whether X" is legitimate and useful. Neither dress a suspicion as fact nor
  drop it.

Order by what matters most to the reader, not category or chronology. Write
as if over the owner's shoulder: direct, unhedged, no throat-clearing about
what you were asked or how thorough you were.

Nothing in range trips the list → the decision line, then "Nothing to
report.", plus at most one sentence naming the range covered. Do not pad a
clean round into a summary: mundane summary trains the reader to skim you, so
the one time you have something, it gets skimmed too. Silence is the signal.
A weak item beside a real one dilutes the real one.

